UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 13, Lesson 1: Telling a story through drama', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"A story told through drama has a clear structure. The beginning introduces the characters and the place, the middle brings a problem that the characters must solve, and the end shows the resolution, which is how the problem is fixed. Each part can be a scene, a short section in one place. Actors use narration, dialogue (the words characters say) and action to tell the story. Plan the story first by listing who, where, the problem and the solution, so the audience can follow what happens.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same arts idea to a new artwork or performance, then compare two choices for clarity of effect.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nPlan a short drama about a missing football at the park. Write the who, where, problem and resolution in order.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Who: two friends. Where: the park. Problem: the football rolled into the bushes. Resolution: they asked a gardener and worked together to find it.'' It has a beginning, a clear problem and a satisfying end.\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking that a drama story only needs lots of action. Without a clear problem and resolution, the audience loses the thread, so planning the structure matters more than adding more events.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nMake a story plan card for a drama of your own with a title, characters, setting, problem and resolution. Add one line of narration and two lines of dialogue. Tell your plan to a family member and ask what they think will happen next, to see whether the problem is clear.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one artistic choice you changed and why. Movement or instrument work is optional, adult-supervised and can be described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Creative Arts","learning_intention":"A story told through drama has a clear structure. The beginning introduces the characters and the place, the middle brings a problem that the characters must solve, and the end shows the resolution, which is how the problem is fixed. Each part can be a scene, a short section in one place. Actors use narration, dialogue (the words characters say) and action to tell the story. Plan the story first by listing who, where, the problem and the solution, so the audience can follow what happens.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Explore and learn","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: scrap paper, crayons, safe classroom instruments or clear movement space with an adult nearby"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["CA2-DRA-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Make a story plan card for a drama of your own with a title, characters, setting, problem and resolution. Add one line of narration and two lines of dialogue. Tell your plan to a family member and ask what they think wil Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Strong answer: ''Who: two friends. Where: the park. Problem: the football rolled into the bushes. Resolution: they asked a gardener and worked together to find it.'' It has a beginning, a clear… Reasoning: I used a story beginning by attending to a problem rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Thinking that a drama story only needs lots of action. Without a clear problem and resolution, the audience loses the thread, so planning the structure… Correction: Strong answer: ''Who: two friends. Where: the park. Problem: the football rolled into the bushes. Resolution: they asked a gardener and worked together to find… Why: the correction restores accurate use of a story beginning.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking that a drama story only needs lots of action. Without a clear problem and resolution, the audience loses the thread, so planning the structure matters more than adding more events. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating a story beginning on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with a story beginning, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Strong answer: ''Who: two friends. Where: the park. Problem: the football rolled into the bushes. Resolution: they asked a gardener and worked together to find… This matches the lesson skill for a story beginning.","options":["Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work.","Strong answer: ''Who: two friends. Where: the park. Problem: the football rolled into the bushes. Resolution: they asked a gardener and worked together to find…","Thinking that a drama story only needs lots of action. Without a clear problem and resolution, the audience loses the thread, so planning the structure…"],"question":"Plan a short drama about a missing football at the park. Write the who, where, problem and resolution in order. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Thinking that a drama story only needs lots of action. Without a clear problem and resolution, the audience loses the thread, so planning the structure… That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Careful use of a problem with clear evidence.","Thinking that a drama story only needs lots of action. Without a clear problem and resolution, the audience loses the thread, so planning the structure…","A story told through drama has a clear structure. The beginning introduces the characters and the place, the middle brings a problem that the characters must…","Strong answer: ''Who: two friends. Where: the park. Problem: the football rolled into the bushes. Resolution: they asked a gardener and worked together to find…"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising a story beginning?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named a problem, not by copying the model.","options":["Apply the idea with new evidence and name the a problem used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Invent a sacred First Nations design without permission or guidance."],"question":"A learner is practising a story beginning. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Strong answer: ''Who: two friends. Where: the park. Problem: the football rolled into the bushes. Resolution: they asked a gardener and worked together to find… uses elements, techniques and audience effect appropriately for this lesson.","options":["Thinking that a drama story only needs lots of action. Without a clear problem and resolution, the audience loses the thread, so planning the structure…","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work.","Strong answer: ''Who: two friends. Where: the park. Problem: the football rolled into the bushes. Resolution: they asked a gardener and worked together to find…"],"question":"Which response best shows careful thinking about a story beginning?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows.","options":["Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail.","Thinking that a drama story only needs lots of action. Without a clear problem and resolution, the audience loses the thread, so planning the structure…","Name a specific source, cue or example and say what it shows about a story beginning.","Make a bigger claim without naming any source or cue."],"question":"Which habit strengthens evidence about a problem?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail.","options":["Thinking that a drama story only needs lots of action. Without a clear problem and resolution, the audience loses the thread, so planning the structure…","Invent a sacred First Nations design without permission or guidance.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Example: ''The Lost Lantern''. Beginning: two children camp in a dark forest. Problem: their lantern is missing. Middle: they search…"],"question":"Using this lesson idea — A story told through drama has a clear structure. The beginning introduces the characters and the place, the… — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Respect and accuracy mean checking evidence and honouring limits/protocols.","options":["Check facts/cues, acknowledge limits, and avoid inventing details.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete.","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster.","Treat every online claim as equally reliable."],"question":"Which action shows respect and accuracy together?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the skill.","options":["Retype the worked example with no new example.","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work.","Thinking that a drama story only needs lots of action. Without a clear problem and resolution, the audience loses the thread, so planning the structure…","Make a story plan card for a drama of your own with a title, characters, setting, problem and resolution. Add one line of narration and two lines of…"],"question":"Which task best shows independent use of a story beginning?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating a story beginning on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Strong answer: ''Who: two friends. Where: the park. Problem: the football rolled into the bushes. Resolution: they asked a gardener and worked together to find it.'' It has a beginning, a clear… Reasoning: I used a story beginning by attending to a problem rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with a story beginning, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Thinking that a drama story only needs lots of action. Without a clear problem and resolution, the audience loses the thread, so planning the structure… Correction: Strong answer: ''Who: two friends. Where: the park. Problem: the football rolled into the bushes. Resolution: they asked a gardener and worked together to find… Why: the correction restores accurate use of a story beginning.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent artwork or performance notes and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-arts-y3-w13-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Creative Arts alignment using catalogue outcome codes (CA2-*); content-point mapping remains pending. First Nations arts content requires local Aboriginal educator or Custodian guidance before acceptance; do not invent sacred designs.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise a story beginning.","Read the mission before you start making or performing.","Keep the audience effect of your choice in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not name an effect clearly."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the element or technique that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore elements, safety or respectful practice.","Prefer a named artistic reason over a guess that merely looks colourful."],"submit":["Confirm that your choice and reason are both present.","Proofread element names and safety notes.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact element, technique or cue before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key artistic choices.","Say what each choice does for the artwork or audience.","A final praise word alone is weaker than a named effect."],"teach2":["Compare a weak choice with a stronger one.","Change the element or technique, not only the decoration.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh subject or piece that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original work notes and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill on new material."]},"subject":"Drama","success_criteria":["I can use a story beginning with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify a problem and explain why it matters.","I can practise a resolution independently on a new prompt.","I can notice a scene needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"A story told through drama has a clear structure. The beginning introduces the characters and the place, the middle brings a problem that the characters must solve, and the end shows the resolution, which is how the problem is fixed. Each part can be a scene, a short section in…","options":["A story told through drama has a clear structure. The beginning introduces the characters and the place, the middle brings a problem that the characters must solve, and the end shows the resolution, which is how the problem is fixed. Each part can be a scene, a short section in one place. Actors use narration, dialogue (the words characters say) and action to tell the story. Plan the story first by listing who, where, the problem and the solution, so the audience can follow what happens.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a story beginning?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: ''The Lost Lantern''. Beginning: two children camp in a dark forest. Problem: their lantern is missing. Middle: they search together, argue, then work as a team. Resolution: they find the lantern under a log and share their dinner. Plan: Who? Two children. Where? Forest. Problem? Lantern lost. Solution? Teamwork and a careful search.\n\nTRY FIRST\nPlan a short drama about a missing football at the park. Write the who, where, problem and resolution in order.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Who: two friends. Where: the park. Problem: the football rolled into the bushes. Resolution: they asked a gardener and worked together to find it.'' It has a beginning, a clear problem and a satisfying end.","explain":"A story told through drama has a clear structure. The beginning introduces the characters and the place, the middle brings a problem that the characters must solve, and the end shows the resolution, which is how the problem is fixed. Each part can be a scene, a short section in one place. Actors use narration, dialogue (the words characters say) and action to tell the story. Plan the story first by listing who, where, the problem and the solution, so the audience can follow what happens.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Thinking that a drama story only needs lots of action. Without a clear problem and resolution, the audience loses the thread, so planning the structure matters more than adding more events. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","