hnique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a story beginning?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: ''The Lost Lantern''. Beginning: two children camp in a dark forest. Problem: their lantern is missing. Middle: they search together, argue, then work as a team. Resolution: they find the lantern under a log and share their dinner. Plan: Who? Two children. Where? Forest. Problem? Lantern lost. Solution? Teamwork and a careful search. Look at each line, sound, movement or role choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key artistic choices from the example and say what each one does for the audience or artwork.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful response names the element or technique and its effect, not only “it looks nice”.","explain":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final praise word without naming a choice does not show arts thinking. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Show the artistic choice","visual":[{"alt":"Middle and resolution.","caption":"End with a resolution.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Middle</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">characters search</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">End</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">problem is solved</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect.","options":["A guess with no artistic reason.","Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a story beginning?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThinking that a drama story only needs lots of action. Without a clear problem and resolution, the audience loses the thread, so planning the structure matters more than adding more events. Here is a more helpful example: Example: ''The Lost Lantern''. Beginning: two children camp in a dark forest. Problem: their lantern is missing. Middle: they search together, argue, then work as a team. Resolution: they find the lantern under a log and share their dinner. Plan: Who? Two children. Where? Forest. Problem? Lantern lost. Solution? Teamwork and a careful search.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice weakens the work, then write a stronger choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Who: two friends. Where: the park. Problem: the football rolled into the bushes. Resolution: they asked a gardener and worked together to find it.'' It has a beginning, a clear problem and a satisfying end.","explain":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the decoration without changing the artistic idea does not fix the error. Thinking that a drama story only needs lots of action. Without a clear problem and resolution, the audience loses the thread, so planning the structure matters more than adding more events. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect.","options":["A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason.","Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a story beginning?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA story told through drama has a clear structure. The beginning introduces the characters and the place, the middle brings a problem that the characters must solve, and the end shows the resolution, which is how the problem is fixed. Each part can be a scene, a short section in one place. Actors use narration, dialogue (the words characters say) and action to tell the story. Plan the story first by listing who, where, the problem and the solution, so the audience can follow what happens. Now choose a different subject or piece to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same arts idea as the model, but with different content or material.","explain":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new artwork or performance. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Transfer to a new artwork or performance"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 13, Lesson 1: Telling a story through drama","unit_number":3,"unit_title":"Role, character and storytelling","week":13,"week_in_unit":3,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: ''The Lost Lantern''. Beginning: two children camp in a dark forest. Problem: their lantern is missing. Middle: they search together, argue, then work as a team. Resolution: they find the lantern under a log and share their dinner. Plan: Who? Two children. Where? Forest. Problem? Lantern lost. Solution? Teamwork and a careful search.\n\nWrite what makes this example effective, naming the element, technique or performance choice and its effect.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Parts of a drama story.","caption":"Plan the parts.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Beginning</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">who and where</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Problem</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">what goes wrong</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Middle and resolution.","caption":"End with a resolution.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Middle</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">characters search</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">End</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">problem is solved</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 3"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-arts-y3-w13-l1' AND family_id IS NULL;
